§ CSLv3 REFERENCE COMPILER
I> CSSL is a separate project ¬ part-of-CSLv3
I> this spec = reference compiler/parser for CSLv3 notation only
I> ingests CSLv3 source → typed-AST → IR → x86-64 ∨ SPIR-V
I> CSLv3 executable subset = notation that reads as code + spec simultaneously
R! ← Forth/Factor: stack-based, concatenative
R! ← APL: rank-polymorphic operators
R! ← Agda: dependent types as specs

§ SCOPE
  ✗ this spec does ¬ describe CSSL (separate language, separate repo)
  ✓ this spec describes the reference compiler for CSLv3 executable subset
  ✓ parser implementation: C:\Users\Apocky\source\repos\CSLv3\parser\

§ PIPELINE
  source.csl
    → lexer        § §01 glyph table + §02 token classes
    → parser       § §02 BNF → AST
    → semantic     § alias-expand + suffix-resolve + morpheme-detect
    → type-check   § §07 type system → typed-AST
    → IR-lowering  § typed-AST → SSA + regions
    → target-lowering:
        x86-64: SSA → register alloc → machine code (AVX2)
        SPIR-V: SSA → SPIR-V module (Vulkan 1.3 compute+graphics)
    → link → executable ∨ shader-module

§ LEXICAL
  token ← glyph | word | num | str | suffix | punct
  whitespace = separator (except in strings)
  comments: # ... EOL  ∨  §{ ... §}

§ CORE FORMS
  decl     : name : type = expr
  fn-def   : fn name ⟨params⟩ → ret = body
  type-def : def name = ⟨fields⟩ ∨ enum[variants]
  import   : use §module.name
  export   : pub name
  alias    : alias short = long.compound.path

§ EXPRESSION GRAMMAR (CFG)
  expr     ::= atom | unary | binary | call | lambda | pipeline | block
  atom     ::= num | str | ident | glyph-literal
  unary    ::= (¬ | ~ | ! | -) expr
  binary   ::= expr op expr
  call     ::= expr ⟨args⟩
  lambda   ::= λ ⟨params⟩ → expr  |  \ (params) -> expr
  pipeline ::= expr (|> expr)+
  block    ::= { stmt* expr? }
  match    ::= match expr { (pattern → expr)+ (* → expr)? }

§ TYPE SYSTEM (see §07 for full inventory)
  primitives:  ɴ{8,16,32,64} ꜱ ʙ ᴠ⟨T⟩ ᴍ⟨K,V⟩
  ASCII alias: u8..u64 i8..i64 f32 f64 str bool
  algebraic:   sum (T₁ | T₂) product ⟨f₁:T₁, f₂:T₂⟩
  dependent:   Π⟨x:T⟩ → U(x)     # pi type
               Σ⟨x:T, y:U(x)⟩    # sigma type
  refinement:  { x:T ⊢ P(x) }    # subset type
  linear:      T!lin              # must-use-once
  shared:      T&                 # borrowed ref
  exclusive:   T&mut              # exclusive borrow

§ BACKEND TARGETS
  target₁: x86-64 (SysV ABI, AVX2 min ; N! AVX-512 — hw constraint)
  target₂: SPIR-V (Vulkan 1.3, compute+graphics)
  IR:      SSA + regions (MLIR-inspired); lowering passes per target

§ RANK POLYMORPHISM (APL lineage)
  scalar op: fn f (x: T) → T
  elevated:  fn f (x: ᴠ⟨T⟩) → ᴠ⟨T⟩     # auto-lifted
  rank-k:    fn f (x: ᴠᵏ⟨T⟩) → ᴠᵏ⟨T⟩
  R! ops broadcast like numpy ; explicit rank annotation when ambiguous

§ OPERATOR OVERLOADING (APL adverbs)
  /   on fn → reduce (fold)
  \   on fn → scan (running accumulation)
  ¨   on fn → each (map)
  ⍟   on fn+fn → compose

  ex: sum = +/          # reduce by addition
      cumsum = +\       # scan by addition
      doubled = (*2)¨   # map: multiply-by-2 each

§ EXAMPLE: VECTOR ADD
  § vec-add.csl
  pub fn add ⟨a: ᴠ⟨ɴ32⟩, b: ᴠ⟨ɴ32⟩⟩ → ᴠ⟨ɴ32⟩ =
    W! |a| = |b|
    a ⊕ b    # elementwise, rank-lifted

  fn main =
    let x = [1,2,3,4] : ᴠ⟨ɴ32⟩
    let y = [10,20,30,40]
    let z = add⟨x, y⟩
    # z = [11,22,33,44]
  ∎

§ EXAMPLE: DAMAGE CALC (from §09)
  § combat.dmg.csl
  fn calc-damage (atk :: Stats, def :: Stats, wpn :: Weapon) → f32 =
    let raw = wpn.dmg * (atk.str / 10 + 1)
    let mit = max(1, raw - def.armor)
    let final = rand(0,1) < atk.crit ? mit * (atk.crit_mult | 2) : mit
    def.hp -= final
    → final
  ∎

§ EXAMPLE: PHYSICS TICK
  fn tick-physics (world :: World, dt :: f32) =
    ∀ entity ∈ world.entities:
      entity.vel += entity.accel * dt
      entity.pos += entity.vel * dt
      entity.accel = vec3(0)  # reset accumulator
  ∎

§ IR DESIGN
  SSA + regions (MLIR-like)
  region = nested scope w/ explicit entry/exit
  phi nodes for control flow merge
  linear type checking at IR level → resource lifecycle verification

  R! IR preserves dependent type info through lowering
  R! IR supports rank polymorphism via shape annotations
  W! IR validates all ⌈constraints⌉ + ⌊preconditions⌋ at compile-time where possible

